#188 landed the agent definitions this will need. Two things for you when you get here: (1) the availability record (agents.state, beside the config) is read-modify-write with no lock -- two overlapping ticks can lose one update, which costs one probe interval today but matters more once the runner is concurrent; App::karr::Foundation::Agents::_read_state/_mutate is the place. (2) the spec's per_agent concurrency limits name the agents defined in that config's 'agents:' section, so Agents->definitions is the list to validate them against rather than a second free-form name space.
#190 landed the sync of refs/karr-foundation/*, but only in `karr sync`. The runner still pulls the board only: App::karr::Foundation::_sync_pull calls $git->pull and nothing else, so a tick reads whatever chain this machine happened to have. The primitives are there -- App::karr::Git::pull_foundation / push_foundation, and App::karr::Role::SyncLifecycle::sync_pull_foundation / sync_push_foundation for the retrying versions. A tick that executes a step should pull the fleet namespace before it reads the chain and push it after it has written the step state and the run log, or two machines will happily run the same step.
Concurrent runner + three concurrency levels. Commit 5a4df4c (local, not pushed).

WHAT LANDED
- New App::karr::Foundation::Limits resolves the three levels, tightest wins: config concurrent: (machine ceiling, default 1), concurrent: on a named agent definition (operator estimate), and the chain header's limits: {concurrent, per_agent}. per_agent names are checked against Agents->definitions (#188's note); an agent this machine does not define is dropped with a --verbose note, not refused, because agent definitions are local by design. A bad number is user_error in the local config and warn+ignore from the header.
- Foundation::run builds the plan once (_plan_repos), pulls refs/karr-foundation/* from the configured hub: before the header is read (#190's note; nothing is pushed back, this runner writes no step state), then either _run_serially (ceiling 1, and always under --dry-run) or _run_concurrent.
- _run_concurrent forks one child per board running the whole of _process_repo. Boards that will not start an agent (disabled, no agent, unknown agent name) are handled in the parent before the first fork. The queue is scanned rather than shifted, so a board at its agent's limit does not block the ones behind it.
- Shutdown: the parent TERMs its children and each child runs the #148/#163 path for its own board (TERM then KILL to the agent's process group, release its lock); KILL only for a child that did not manage that. A child clears the inherited sibling list and lock fds at birth and leaves through POSIX::_exit.
- Agents::_mutate now reads and writes agents.state inside one flock on a sibling agents.state.lock.

